To: Operations Leadership Team
From: Supply Chain Consultant
Subject: Warehouse network decision — which sites to open and how to serve customers

We’re at a point where we need to decide which of our seven potential warehouse sites to open and how to flow product to our forty customer locations. The objective is straightforward: meet every customer’s needs at the lowest possible total cost, balancing the fixed cost to open facilities with the variable cost of shipping product.

What makes this decision meaningful is the trade-off between proximity and overhead. Opening more sites brings inventory closer to customers and can reduce transport spend, but it also adds fixed cost and ties up capacity that we may not need. Opening fewer sites saves on overhead, but it can push up shipping distances and risks overloading the facilities we do open. Any site that we choose not to open cannot ship product. At the same time, every customer’s demand must be fully satisfied without exceeding the throughput limits of the facilities we operate.

Data available for this decision:
- Candidate warehouse map coordinates: {FACILITY_COORDINATES}
- Customer map coordinates: {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}
- Warehouse capacities (maximum volume each site can handle): {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}
- Customer demand requirements: {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}
- Fixed opening costs by site: {FIXED_COSTS}
- Transport rate per unit and distance: {TRANSPORT_RATE}

How we’ll frame the decision:
- Shipping costs depend on how far each facility is from each customer and how much volume we move. Closer pairings generally lower transport spend, but only if the facility has capacity and the savings outweigh the fixed cost of opening it.
- We will determine a combination of sites to open that provides enough capacity to cover all customer demand while keeping total cost down.
- For the open network, we will assign customer volumes to facilities in a way that respects each site’s capacity and ensures every customer is fully served.
- The end result will be a recommended set of warehouse openings and a shipping plan that together minimize total cost (facility opening costs plus shipping costs), along with a clear explanation of the trade-offs behind the recommendation.
